div.p:
The operator, whether sitting or standing, should be positioned conveniently with respect to himself, to the part being operated on, and to the light. Now of light there are two kinds, natural and artificial; the natural is not in our power, whereas the artificial is in our power. Each of these has two uses: direct light or oblique light. Oblique light is little used, and its proper degree is obvious. As for direct light, from the available and suitable lights, turn the part being operated on toward the brightest—except such parts as ought to be concealed or are indecent to expose. Thus the part operated on should face the light, and the operator should face the part operated on, except so as not to cast a shadow;
for in that way the operator will see, while the part operated on will not be exposed to view. With respect to himself, when seated, his feet should be in a straight line directly beneath his knees, and slightly apart; his knees a little higher than the groins, and spaced apart to support and rest his elbows. His garment should be neat, orderly, even, and uniform over elbows and shoulders. With respect to the part operated on: far and near, above and below, to one side or the other, or in the middle. The limit of far and near is that the elbows should not pass forward beyond the knees nor backward beyond the sides; of above, not to hold the hands higher than the breasts;
of below, not lower than where, with the chest resting on the knees, the hands are at right angles to the upper arms. So much for the middle position; as for to one side or the other, he should not go beyond his seat, but shift his body and the working part of his body in proportion to the turn. When standing, he should inspect while standing evenly on both feet, well balanced, but operate with his weight on one foot—not the one on the side of the operating hand; the height of the knee to the groins as when seated, and the other limits the same. The patient should assist the operator with the other part of his body, whether standing, sitting, or lying, so as most easily to maintain the required posture, guarding against slipping,
sinking, twisting, and leaning, so that both the posture and the form of the part operated on are preserved during presentation, operation, and subsequent positioning.

The nails should neither project beyond nor fall short of the fingertips;
use is made of the tips of the fingers. Practice with the fingertips, mostly the index finger against the thumb; with the whole hand prone; with both hands opposed. A good natural conformation of the fingers is a wide space between the fingers and the thumb opposed to the index finger. It is clear that it is a harmful defect in those who from birth or upbringing are accustomed to hold the thumb down under the other
fingers. Practice all operations with each hand separately and with both together (for both are alike), aiming to act well, neatly, quickly, painlessly, gracefully, and readily.
